; @CALCULUS@: a starting signature, with one rule.
;
; A starting point for a new one, not a calculus anyone should keep. It has the
; two operators the checker layer requires and one rule, which is enough to
; write a proof that refutes its assumptions -- and so enough to exercise the
; whole pipeline: parser, checker, semantics, verdict.
;
; `Bool`, `true` and `false` are Eunoia builtins and need no declaration.

; Required by the checker layer. `and` must carry `:right-assoc-nil true`
; because rules gathering `:list` premises build the list through its nil; this
; calculus has no such rule, but keeping the attribute means adding one later
; does not turn into a puzzle.
(declare-const and (-> Bool Bool Bool) :right-assoc-nil true)

; Not required by the checker -- only by rules. This one needs it.
(declare-const not (-> Bool Bool))

; The one rule: from a formula and its negation, derive false.
;
; Enough to make a refutation possible, which is what the checker is *for*: its
; verdict is whether the proof reached `false`.
(declare-rule contra ((F Bool))
  :premises (F (not F))
  :conclusion false
)
